{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Recipe",
  "name": "Crunchy Dumpling Lettuce Wraps",
  "description": "Korean-inspired lettuce wraps with a Jain TVP filling, homemade gochujang, and crunchy dumpling strips.",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "dhanjas.com"
  },
  "recipeCategory": "Snack",
  "recipeCuisine": "Indian (Jain)",
  "suitableForDiet": "https://schema.org/VegetarianDiet",
  "keywords": "TVP · Fusion, High-protein, Air fryer, Jain Vegetarian, Toddler-adapted",
  "recipeIngredient": [
    "150g Dry TVP (granules/chunks)",
    "2 cups Hot water",
    "2 tbsp Neutral oil",
    "2 stalks Celery, sliced thin diagonal",
    "~4 cups Green cabbage, shredded",
    "1 medium Red bell pepper, julienned",
    "1 small Carrot, julienned (toddler only)",
    "6½ tbsp Reduced-sodium soy sauce",
    "3 tbsp + 1 tsp Fresh ginger",
    "¾ tsp Hing",
    "1¼ tsp Sugar",
    "4 tbsp Light brown sugar or jaggery",
    "1 tsp Salt",
    "5 tbsp Gochugaru (Korean chili flakes)",
    "2 tbsp White miso paste",
    "1 tbsp Rice vinegar",
    "3 tbsp Warm water",
    "⅓ cup Neutral oil (peanut or canola)",
    "3 tbsp Sesame seeds",
    "2 tbsp Roasted peanuts, crushed",
    "6 tbsp Water",
    "2 tbsp Toasted sesame oil",
    "1 tbsp Cornstarch",
    "2 tbsp Jain gochujang, from the batch above",
    "½ tsp Ghee (toddler)",
    "14 Eggless dumpling wrappers",
    "1 tbsp Neutral oil, for the strips",
    "¼ tsp White pepper",
    "2 heads Butter lettuce",
    "¼ cup Celery leaves or coriander",
    "Jain chili crisp, from the batch above, to taste"
  ],
  "recipeInstructions": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToSection",
      "name": "Soak the TVP, make the gochujang and chili crisp",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Stir hot water (2 cups), soy sauce (1½ tbsp), grated ginger (1 tsp), hing (¼ tsp) and sugar (½ tsp) together in a large bowl; add TVP (150g), stir well, cover and set aside 15–20 min — it returns in Step 3 with all its liquid"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Gochujang: in a small bowl, whisk gochugaru (3 tbsp) (or 2 tbsp Kashmiri chili powder + 1 tbsp regular chili powder), white miso (2 tbsp), brown sugar (2 tbsp), rice vinegar (1 tbsp) and salt (¼ tsp)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Add warm water (3 tbsp) a spoonful at a time, whisking, until a thick glossy paste falls slowly off the spoon; rest 10 min in the bowl — makes about ⅔ cup, you need 2 tbsp today and the rest keeps a month refrigerated"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Chili crisp: put gochugaru (2 tbsp), sesame seeds (1 tbsp), crushed peanuts (2 tbsp), hing (¼ tsp), sugar (½ tsp) and salt (½ tsp) into a heatproof bowl"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Heat neutral oil (⅓ cup) with julienned ginger (1 tbsp) in a small pan over medium heat, 4–5 min, until the ginger is pale gold and the bubbling slows"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Off the heat, wait 30 sec, then pour the hot contents of the pan over the bowl — it sizzles hard; stir and cool completely (it goes on at serving)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToSection",
      "name": "Mix the sauce, prep, crisp the strips",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Whisk soy sauce (5 tbsp), water (6 tbsp), toasted sesame oil (2 tbsp), brown sugar (2 tbsp) and cornstarch (1 tbsp) in a bowl until completely smooth"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Scoop (=3 tbsp) of this plain sauce into a small bowl and set it aside for the toddler; then stir Jain gochujang (2 tbsp) into the big bowl"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Slice celery (2 stalks) thin on a sharp diagonal — long shards, not chunks; shred cabbage (~4 cups), julienne red bell pepper (1 medium), grate ginger (2 tbsp)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Julienne carrot (1 small) for the toddler; microwave covered with a splash of water, 2 min, until just tender; drain"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Stack dumpling wrappers (14) and cut into strips about ½ cm wide; separate, wash and thoroughly dry butter lettuce (2 heads) into cups — wet leaves make the filling slide out"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Toss the strips with oil (1 tbsp) until barely slicked; air-fry in a single layer in the Ninja Crispi at 330°F for 4–6 min, shaking at 3 min — pull them while still pale gold"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Set a small handful of plain strips aside for the toddler, salted very lightly; toss the rest with white pepper (¼ tsp), sugar (¼ tsp) and salt (¼ tsp) while still warm"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToSection",
      "name": "Cook the filling",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Heat neutral oil (2 tbsp) in a large sauté pan over medium-high heat"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Add hing (¼ tsp) and swirl for 10 seconds until it smells savory and nutty — don't let it brown"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Add celery (2 stalks) and grated ginger (2 tbsp); cook 2 minutes — the celery should stay squeaky, not go soft"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Add cabbage (~4 cups) and red bell pepper (1 medium); cook 5–7 minutes until the cabbage wilts and the pepper just softens"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Add the soaked TVP (150g) from Step 1 with all its remaining liquid; cook 2 minutes. Stop here — nothing spicy has gone in yet."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToSection",
      "name": "👶 Toddler — pull her filling now, before the gochujang sauce",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Scoop about 1 cup of the filling out of the pan into a small saucepan"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Add her softened carrot (1 small) and the plain reserved sauce (3 tbsp)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Simmer 2 minutes, stirring, until it thickens and coats everything; finish off the heat with ghee (½ tsp)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Serve with her plain salted strips, and torn butter lettuce or a small bowl of rice"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Hing and ginger are fine for her — she's only missing the gochujang and the chili crisp"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToSection",
      "name": "Finish and assemble",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Pour the big bowl of spiced sauce from Step 2 over what's left in the pan; cook 5–7 minutes, stirring, until glossy and thick enough to sit in a lettuce leaf"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Taste before adding salt — there's soy in both the soak and the sauce; it should be sweet-salty first, heat second"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Spoon the filling into the lettuce cups"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Top each with the seasoned strips, toasted sesame seeds (2 tbsp) and chopped celery leaves (¼ cup)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "text": "Finish with Jain chili crisp (to taste); add the strips at the table — they go soft within minutes of touching the sauce"
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToTip",
          "text": "What changed from the original: onion → celery, sliced thin, plus hing bloomed in the oil. Garlic → hing. Green onion → celery leaves or coriander. Carrot → red bell pepper for adults (real carrot stays only in the toddler's portion). Bouillon cube → soy sauce + ginger + hing in the soaking water — cubes are rarely Jain. Gochujang and chili crisp are made from scratch because most jarred brands, Lao Gan Ma included, aren't Jain. \"Chicken spice\" → white pepper, sugar, salt. Buy Chinese-style wrappers (flour, water, salt) — some gyoza wrappers aren't eggless, so check the packet. The Crispi runs hot: 330°F is its floor and plenty here — strips go from pale to burnt in about 45 seconds. No air fryer? Deep-fry the strips in batches in 2 cups of oil until golden. Use a wide sauté pan, not a wok, so the cabbage wilts fast instead of steaming. The gochujang improves overnight. No wrappers? Cut flour tortillas or spring roll pastry into strips. Leftover filling keeps 3 days refrigerated; keep strips and chili crisp at room temperature. For a milder version, halve the gochujang or serve it on the side."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "datePublished": "2026-09-07",
  "recipeYield": "4 servings",
  "totalTime": "PT50M",
  "nutrition": {
    "@type": "NutritionInformation",
    "proteinContent": "20 g",
    "description": "~20g protein per adult serving — from TVP, plus peanuts in the chili crisp"
  }
}
